---
layout: base.njk
title: Start your studio
permalink: /checkout/
description: "Start your Prospektor workspace: $999 a month, unlimited runs, your whole team in. Next stop is Stripe, and the research runs while you are still paying."
---

<section class="onboard">
  <div class="onboard-inner">

    <ol class="steps" id="steps">
      <li class="step done"><span class="step-n-badge">✓</span>Scan</li>
      <li class="step active" data-step="target"><span class="step-n-badge">2</span>Your target</li>
      <li class="step" data-step="pay"><span class="step-n-badge">3</span>Payment</li>
      <li class="step" data-step="signin"><span class="step-n-badge">4</span>Sign in</li>
    </ol>

    <!-- STEP 2: confirm the target -->
    <div class="onboard-panel" id="panelTarget">
      <div class="scan-result-meta" id="obMeta"></div>
      {#- One <h1> per document (#137). These are three steps of one flow, only one
        of them visible at a time, but a crawler sees the whole page at once and
        read three competing <h1>s. The class carries the styling, so the steps
        look identical. -#}
      <h1 class="onboard-h1">Here's what Prospektor will hunt for you.</h1>
      <p class="onboard-sub">Read it, sharpen it, or rewrite it in your own words — this sentence seeds your studio's brief, and you stay its author.</p>
      <!-- Direct path (no scan): the studio researches the buyer from their
           site, so the one thing we must have is their address. -->
      <div id="siteAsk" hidden>
        <p class="onboard-sub">Came here directly? All we need is your website — your studio researches you from it. (Or <a href="/#scan">scan it first</a> to see what it finds, free.)</p>
        <input class="reserve-input" id="siteInput" type="text" inputmode="url" autocomplete="url"
               placeholder="yourcompany.com" aria-label="Your website">
        <p class="reserve-msg is-error" id="siteMsg" hidden></p>
      </div>
      <textarea class="goal-input" id="goalInput" rows="4" placeholder="What should Prospektor find for you? e.g. Mid-size property managers in the DACH region who still screen tenants manually."></textarea>
      <div class="onboard-actions">
        <button class="btn-cta" id="toPayBtn">Looks right — continue →</button>
        <a class="onboard-back" href="/#scan">← rescan a different site</a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 3: payment -->
    <div class="onboard-panel" id="panelPay" hidden>
      <h2 class="onboard-h1">Payment.</h2>
      <p class="onboard-sub">One subscription, the whole product. The moment payment lands, your studio starts researching you — it works while you find your way in.</p>
      <div class="order-card">
        <div class="order-row">
          <span class="order-name">Prospektor Studio · one workspace</span>
          <span class="order-price">$999<span class="order-per">/mo</span></span>
        </div>
        <ul class="pricing-list">
          <li>Unlimited pitch runs, under fair use</li>
          <li>Deck, one-pager, cold emails and proposal — every run, in your voice</li>
          <li>Your whole team in via your work-email domain</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <!-- Live checkout, revealed by checkout.js only when the server says
           Stripe keys exist. Until then the founding-spot capture below is
           the payment step, exactly as before. -->
      <div id="stripePay" hidden>
        <form class="reserve-form" id="stripeForm" novalidate>
          <input class="reserve-input" id="payEmail" type="email" autocomplete="email"
                 placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" aria-label="Your work email">
          <button class="btn-cta" type="submit" id="stripeBtn">Continue to secure checkout →</button>
        </form>
        <p class="onboard-sub">This address becomes your studio's sign-in. Use your work email so your whole team can get in — and if you have a promotion code, there's a field for it at checkout.</p>
        <p class="reserve-msg is-error" id="stripeMsg" hidden></p>
      </div>
      <div class="onboard-note" id="payFallbackNote">
        <p><strong>Checkout isn't open yet — days, not weeks.</strong> Leave your email and we'll hold a founding spot; you'll get one message when checkout opens, nothing else. Your target sentence rides along so your studio starts from it.</p>
      </div>
      <form class="reserve-form" id="reserveForm" novalidate>
        <input class="reserve-input" id="reserveEmail" type="email" autocomplete="email"
               placeholder="you@yourcompany.com" aria-label="Your work email">
        <input type="text" name="hp" id="reserveHp" tabindex="-1" autocomplete="off" style="position:absolute;left:-9999px;" aria-hidden="true">
        <button class="btn-cta" type="submit" id="reserveBtn">Hold my founding spot</button>
      </form>
      <!-- Static twin of the capture form so Netlify registers it at deploy
           time; checkout.js posts to it when the email function fails. -->
      <form name="founding-spot" netlify netlify-honeypot="hp" hidden>
        <input type="email" name="email">
        <input type="text" name="domain">
        <input type="text" name="company">
        <textarea name="goal"></textarea>
        <input type="text" name="hp">
      </form>
      <p class="reserve-msg" id="reserveMsg" hidden></p>
      <div class="onboard-actions">
        <button class="onboard-back" id="toSigninBtn">what happens after payment →</button>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- STEP 4: sign in -->
    <div class="onboard-panel" id="panelSignin" hidden>
      <h2 class="onboard-h1">Sign in — the studio is already yours.</h2>
      <p class="onboard-sub">After payment you get one email. It says: go to <a href="https://studio.prospektor.ai">studio.prospektor.ai</a> and sign in with the address you paid with — with Google, or with a single-use link the studio emails you. No token, no setup wizard.</p>
      <ul class="pricing-list">
        <li>While you paid, the studio researched you — you arrive to a drafted brief, not an empty page</li>
        <li>You confirm your target sentence (the one from the last step) once</li>
        <li>Three researched prospects are waiting to run their first pitches</li>
        <li>Use your work email and every colleague on your domain can sign straight in</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="onboard-actions">
        <button class="btn-cta" id="backToPayBtn">← back to payment</button>
      </div>
    </div>

  </div>
</section>
{#- defer (#137). This sat mid-document without it, so the parser stopped
    here and rendered nothing below until the script had been fetched and
    run. Every one of these is an IIFE that looks its elements up by id and
    returns if they are absent, so deferring is not only cheaper — it is the
    case the guard was written for, with the DOM guaranteed complete. -#}
<script src="/assets/js/checkout.js" defer></script>
